### 2.4. Catalytic oxidation of TMB by CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites

The peroxidase-like activity of the CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites was evaluated in measuring the catalytic capability of the peroxidase substrate TMB•2HCl in the presence of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. In a series of representative experiments, 0.2 mL of TMB (1 mM) in sodium acetate buffer solution (pH 3.8) was dealt with different reaction systems, listed in Table 1. Each of systems, the total volume 2 mL, was measured by monitoring the absorbance (at 637 nm) in time-scan mode and absorption spectra from 300 to 800 nm, respectively.

### 2.5. Kinetics measurements

The reaction kinetic measurements were recorded in time course mode on a UV–vis absorbance spectrophotometer. Catalytic experiments were carried out as follows: 0.3 mg mL<sup>−1</sup> CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites in HAc-NaAc buffer solution (pH 3.8) treated with 0.2 mL of TMB (0.1–1 mM) as a substrate and 0.2 mL of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> (0.25 M), or 0.2 mL of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> (10–1000 mM) as a substrate and TMB (1 mM) with a total reaction volume of 2 mL at room temperature. The Michaelis–Menten constant ($K_m$) was calculated by the Lineweaver–Burk plot: $1/v = K_m/V_{max} (1/[S] + 1/K_m)$, where $v$ is the initial velocity, $V_{max}$ is the maximal reaction velocity, $K_m$ is the Michaelis constant and $[S]$ is the concentration of the substrate [39].

### 2.6. Detection of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> using CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites

The colorimetric detection of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> using CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites as catalysts was investigated by measuring capability of the catalytic oxidation of the peroxidase substrate TMB (0.2 mL, 1 mM) in the presence of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> (0.2 mL), together with 1.4 mL sodium acetate buffer solution (pH 3.8) and 0.2 mL of CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites solution (0.3 mg·mL<sup>−1</sup>).

## 3. Results and discussion

### 3.1. Structure characterization

Fig. 1 represents the XRD patterns of MMT (a), CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs (b), and CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites (c), respectively. Fig. 1b shows the XRD patterns of the as-prepared CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs without MMT as a support. The characteristic planes (1 1 1), (2 0 0), (2 2 0), (3 1 1), (4 0 0), (4 2 0) were attributable to the face-centred cubic fluorite structure of CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs (JCPDS34-0394), which shows spiculate peaks related to the high crystallinity. From Fig. 1c, it can be seen that the characteristic but relatively weak peaks (marked with *) at 2θ = 28.5°, 32.9°, 47.4°, 56.8° of CeO<sub>2</sub> from the CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites were in accordance with pure CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs (Fig. 1b), suggesting that CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs were deposited on the layers of MMT.

The microstructure and morphology of the CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites (Fig. 2A), the pure CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs (Fig. 3A) and hybrid materials (Fig. 3B) were imaged by TEM. From Fig. 2A, it can be seen that the CeO<sub>2</sub> nanoparticles with the mean size of ca. 10–25 nm were well decorated on the layers of MMT. The HRTEM image shown in Fig. 2B reveals clear lattice fringes with a lattice spacing of 1.63 nm corresponding to (1 1 1) plane of CeO<sub>2</sub>. However, the pure CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs without MMT were easy to aggregate (Fig. 3A), due to the absence of a support. Moreover, the aggregated CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs had a relatively better physical dispersity on the layers of MMT by employing MMT as the support under continuous stirring for a relatively long time at room temperature, shown in Fig. 3B. The dispersed CeO<sub>2</sub> nanoparticles on layers of MMT are name as the CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanohybrids.

**Fig. 1.** The XRD spectra of MMT (a), CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs (b) and CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites (c), respectively.

**Fig. 2.** Low magnification (A) and high magnification (B) TEM images of CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites, respectively.

**Fig. 3.** TEM images of CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs (A) and CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanohybrids (B), respectively.

The dispersity and stability of different materials dispersed in aqueous solution were investigated. From Fig. 4, it can be observed that the successive sequence of the dispersity as well as stability in aqueous solution is CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT composites (Fig. 4d) > CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT hybrid material (Fig. 4c) > MMT (Fig. 4b) > CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs (Fig. 4a), which is further verified by the Zeta potential (absolute value), shown in Table 2. As our known, the bigger the Zeta potential, the better the stability of the materials.

**Fig. 4.** The photographs of the dispersity and stability of different materials dispersed in water initially (A) and after standing for 3 h (B), respectively. The different materials: CeO<sub>2</sub> NPs (a), MMT (b), CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT hybrid materials (c) and CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites (d), respectively.

