It is universally acknowledged that residual H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in the milk should be tightly controlled due to its harm to the skin and the human health, even though H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> can be used as the stabilizer or preservative agent in commercial milk products. So, it is pretty important to detect H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in the milk industry during the course of storing the pasteurized milk for long duration [54]. Here, the practical application in detecting H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> of real milk samples was executed under optimal conditions based on our proposed colorimetric method. Moreover, these analyses were performed for three times under the same conditions by the standard addition method. Table 5 shows the results of residual H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in milk samples. The recovery of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in four milk samples is in the range from 98.4% to 102% and the relative standard deviation (RSD) values were as low as 0.27%, indicating that our proposed colorimetric method demonstrates the feasibility and potential in real sample analysis.

