### 3.5. Mechanism of the peroxidase-like activity of CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites

The previous studies have reported that the catalytic mechanism of nanocomposites is the generation of hydroxyl radicals (•OH) from the catalytic decomposition of acidified H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> [46]. As mentioned above, it is believed that CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites would facilitate the electron transfer between H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> and TMB and then catalyze H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> to decompose into hydroxyl radicals under acidic conditions, which oxidizes TMB into a blue product, as illustrated in Scheme 1. To confirm this assumption, the •OH formation was evaluated by adding a fluorescent probe, the terephthalic acid (TA), into the reaction system of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> + CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposite. The TA molecule easily reacted with •OH to form a strong fluorescent product, 2-hydroxyterephthalic acid (TAOH), which emitted unique fluorescence around 435 nm [47,48]. 0.35 mM TA, 0.25 M H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> and the different amount of CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites have been incubated in HAc-NaAc buffer (pH 3.8) at 40 °C for 15 min. The incubated solutions were used to investigate fluorescence intensity. The results show that the fluorescent intensity of the experimental systems gradually enhanced with the increasing amount of CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites (Fig. 9), suggesting that the more the nanocomposites, the more the produced •OH.

**Fig. 9.** The influence of the CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites on the generation of hydroxyl radical with terephthalic acid (TA) as a fluorescent probe. Reaction conditions: TA (0.35 mM), H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> (0.25 M), and the CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites with different concentration (0–700 μg mL<sup>−1</sup>) as the curves indicated (a–h) were incubated in acetate buffer (pH 3.8, 100 mM) at 40 °C for 15 min.

### 3.6. Detection of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> using CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites

On account of the intrinsic peroxidase-like activity of the CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites is H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> concentration dependent, the system investigated above could be applied to detect H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> with a simple colorimetric method. A typical dose-response curve at 637 nm depending on the concentration of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> from 1 μM to 800 μM under the optimal conditions was exhibited in Fig. 10A. From Fig. 10, it can be found that an obvious trend of absorbance gradually increased with increasing of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> concentration. The corresponding linear calibration plot is shown in Fig. 10B, revealing a good linear relationship between the absorbance of oxidized TMB and the concentration of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in the range of 9–500 μM with a detection limit of 7.8 μM. Furthermore, the visualized variation could be observed by the naked eye, as shown in the inserted photographs of colored products corresponding to the different concentration of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. In addition, we have summarized the detection of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> using CeO<sub>2</sub> and CeO<sub>2</sub>-based peroxidase mimetrics in this study and the previous reports, listed in Table 4. From the reported data and our study in this paper, it can be determined that CeO<sub>2</sub>-MMT nanocomposites have a lower detection limit and a wider dynamic range of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in comparison with other peroxidase mimics by the same simple colorimetric method [12,45,49]. Of course, much lower detection limit of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> based on other nanomaterials as peroxidase were reported, mainly because the different test method, an electrochemical method [50], was used.

