Fig. S9. Effect of antibody concentration (c<sub>ab</sub>) on Zeta potential of the antibody-conjugated Au@Pt<sub>3.15</sub> NPs in water.

Without modification, the Au@Pt<sub>3.15</sub> NPs presented a value of about -32 mV, likely due to existence of remaining negatively-charged reducing agents (e. g., ascorbic acid molecules). Contrarily, as the concentration of antibody (c<sub>ab</sub>) increased, the Zeta potential gradually decreased as a result of positively-charged moieties like amino groups in antibodies, but it was still over -20 mV. The results not only suggest successful conjugation of antibodies onto the Au@Pt<sub>3.15</sub> NPs, but show good stability of the as-resulted nanohybrids.

---

Fig. S10. Effect of antibody concentration on peroxidase-like activity of the antibody-conjugated Au@Pt<sub>3.15</sub> NPs.

---

Fig. S11. Effect of incubation time of S1 protein on the absorbance.

The incubation time of S1 protein was optimized by determining the absorbance of the solution at 450 nm (see details in experimental section). Fig. S9 shows that the absorbance increased with the increase in the incubation time from 0.5 h to 2 h; however, by further extending the incubation time, the absorbance remained almost unchanged.

